      "Lewis and Clark spent the winter of 1805–06 in a log stockade a few kilometres from present-day Astoria, and they did not enjoy it. By their count it rained on all but a dozen of the hundred-odd days they spent at Fort Clatsop; their clothes rotted, fleas infested their furs, and they lived largely on elk and on fish and roots bought from their Clatsop neighbours, who had been trading with European ships at the river mouth for years. Chinook and Clatsop villages controlled the lower Columbia, and their headman Comcomly was a shrewd broker for everyone who arrived by sea.",
